<meta type=article>
  <title | Layer 1 Vocabulary Reference>
  <author | The Enscribe Project>
</meta>

**Layer 1** is the stable output vocabulary every Enscribe document compiles to: a
small set of semantic, mostly custom HTML <cite @whatwg-html> elements that record
what each piece of content *is*. This is the element-by-element reference for that vocabulary — what
each element means, what attributes it accepts, what it can contain, and the
canonical HTML it produces. It is a lookup, not a read-through; jump to the element
you need. For tutorial-style explanations and the shorthand that produces these
elements, see the <a href="authoring-guide.html" | Authoring Guide>.

**How to read the HTML examples.** Each entry shows the *canonical* Layer 1 — the
archival structure. At render time, handlers add presentation that is not part of
the canonical form: numbered blocks (`<figure>`, `<table>`, the theorem family)
gain a label span like `<span class="figure-label">Figure 1.</span>`, a `<note>`
splits into an inline marker plus a collected list at the document's foot, and math
elements fill with KaTeX output. The examples below show the canonical structure;
the label and number are added when the document is rendered. Every element is
custom-element-styleable: CSS can target `<theorem>`, `<section-title>`, and the
rest directly.

The catalog is organized by category: <a href="#cat:struct" | Structural>,
<a href="#cat:meta" | Metadata>, <a href="#cat:inline" | Inline>,
<a href="#cat:block" | Block>, <a href="#cat:math" | Math environments>,
<a href="#cat:frame" | Frameable>, <a href="#cat:thm" | Theorem family>,
<a href="#cat:cite" | Citation>, <a href="#cat:note" | Footnote>,
<a href="#cat:ref" | Cross-reference>, and <a href="#cat:dsl" | DSL> elements.

<# #cat:struct | Structural elements #>

The containers that give a document its shape. Most are produced by the structuring
pass rather than authored directly.

<## #el:article | `<article>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<article>` · **content** structured. The
root of an article-type document. Produced from `<meta type=article>` plus the body
content; wraps an `<article-front>` (front matter) and an `<article-body>`.
**Attributes:** `numbering-style`, `note-position`, `id`, `class`.

```html
<article>
  <article-front>
    <meta data-document-type="article">
      <article-title>The Effect of Elephants on Climate</article-title>
      <author>Jane Goodall</author>
    </meta>
  </article-front>
  <article-body> … </article-body>
</article>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:book" | `<book>`>, <a href="#el:meta" | `<meta>`>,
<a href="#el:section" | `<section>`>.

<## #el:book | `<book>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<book>` · **content** structured. The root of
a book-type document, from `<meta type=book>`. Wraps `<book-front>`, `<book-body>`,
and `<book-back>`. **Attributes:** `numbering-style`, `note-position`, `id`, `class`.

```html
<book>
  <book-front> … </book-front>
  <book-body>
    <book-part book-part-type="chapter"> … </book-part>
  </book-body>
  <book-back> … </book-back>
</book>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:book-part" | `<book-part>`>,
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:book" | Authoring Guide ch. 12>.

<## #el:book-front | `<book-front>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<book-front>` · **content** structured.
A book's front matter. Generated from a `<preface>` authoring tag. Holds the book
`<meta>` and preface content.

<## #el:book-body | `<book-body>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<book-body>` · **content** structured. The
body of a book: a sequence of `<book-part>` chapters. Generated, not authored
directly.

<## #el:book-back | `<book-back>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<book-back>` · **content** structured. A
book's back matter — appendices and the book-wide bibliography. Generated from an
`<appendix>` authoring tag and the bibliography.

<## #el:book-part | `<book-part>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<book-part>` · **content** structured. A
chapter (or other top-level book division). Authored as `<chapter | Title>` (with
`<preface>` and `<appendix>` as the front/back-matter variants); the chapter's
title lands in a nested `<meta><book-part-title>`. A per-chapter `<author>`
overrides the book-level author. **Attributes:** `book-part-type`,
`numbering-style`, `note-position`.

```html
<book-part book-part-type="chapter">
  <meta><book-part-title>Origins</book-part-title></meta>
  <p>Content of the chapter.</p>
</book-part>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:section" | `<section>`>, <a href="#el:author" | `<author>`>.

<## #el:section | `<section>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<section>` (HTML-native) · **content**
structured. A top-level division of an article body or chapter. Closes implicitly
when a peer `<section>` opens — no `</section>` is written. **Authoring:**
`<# Title #>` · `<section | Title>` · `# Title`. **Attributes:** `sec-type`
(→ `data-sec-type`, JATS-aligned), `numbering-style`, `id` (`#sec:name`), `class`.

```html
<section>
  <section-title>Introduction</section-title>
  <p>The paper begins here.</p>
</section>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:sub-section" | `<sub-section>`>,
<a href="#el:section-title" | `<section-title>`>,
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:sections" | Authoring Guide ch. 2>.

<## #el:sub-section | `<sub-section>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<sub-section>` · **content** structured.
A depth-2 division nested in a `<section>`. As `<section>` in every respect, one
level down. **Authoring:** `<## Title ##>` · `<sub-section | Title>` · `## Title`.

```html
<sub-section>
  <sub-section-title>Quantitative analysis</sub-section-title>
  <p>Sub-section content.</p>
</sub-section>
```

<## #el:sub-sub-section | `<sub-sub-section>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<sub-sub-section>` · **content** structured.
The depth-3 division, nested in a `<sub-section>`. The ladder stops here.
**Authoring:** `<### Title ###>` · `<sub-sub-section | Title>` · `### Title`.

```html
<sub-sub-section>
  <sub-sub-section-title>Regression analysis</sub-sub-section-title>
  <p>Detailed methods.</p>
</sub-sub-section>
```

<## #el:section-title | `<section-title>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<section-title>` · **content** prose. The
heading of a `<section>`. Usually generated by title extraction from the section's
pipe content; can be written explicitly. Its siblings `<sub-section-title>` and
`<sub-sub-section-title>` are the depth-2 and depth-3 forms. In render mode a
section title lowers to the appropriate `<h2>`–`<h4>` for its depth.

```html
<section-title>Introduction</section-title>
```

<## #el:sub-section-title | `<sub-section-title>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<sub-section-title>` · **content** prose. The
heading of a `<sub-section>` — `<section-title>` one level down. Usually generated
from the sub-section's pipe content. Lowers to `<h3>` in render mode.

<## #el:sub-sub-section-title | `<sub-sub-section-title>` ##>
**Category** structural · **renders** `<sub-sub-section-title>` · **content** prose.
The heading of a `<sub-sub-section>`, the depth-3 title. Lowers to `<h4>`.

<# #cat:meta | Metadata elements #>

The document's apparatus — identity and settings. These are canonical-only (no
sigil or markdown shorthand) and sit at the document's edges.

<## #el:meta | `<meta>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<meta>` · **content** structured. The
document's front-matter block, pinned to the top. `type` picks the document shape.
Its children (`<title>`, `<author>`, `<date>`, `<subtitle>`) become the front
matter. **Attributes:** `type` (`article` | `book`, → `data-document-type`).

```html
<meta data-document-type="article">
  <article-title>The Effect of Elephants on Climate</article-title>
  <author>Jane Goodall</author>
  <date>2024-03-15</date>
</meta>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:title" | `<title>`>, <a href="#el:config" | `<config>`>,
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:structure" | Authoring Guide ch. 1>.

<## #el:title | `<title>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<article-title>` / `<book-title>` · **content**
prose. The document title, written `<title | …>` inside `<meta>`. Renders as
`<article-title>` in an article and `<book-title>` in a book.

```html
<article-front><article-title>The Effect of Elephants on Climate</article-title></article-front>
```

<## #el:subtitle | `<subtitle>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<article-subtitle>` / `<book-subtitle>` ·
**content** prose. An optional secondary title, written `<subtitle | …>` inside
`<meta>`.

```html
<article-subtitle>A Multi-Year Field Study in Tanzania</article-subtitle>
```

<## #el:author | `<author>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<author>` · **content** structured. A document
or chapter author. **Attributes:** `name`, `affiliation`, `orcid`, `email`,
`corresponding` — for a structured author record; the simple form puts the name in
the pipe.

```html
<author>Jane Goodall</author>
```

<## #el:date | `<date>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<date>` · **content** prose. A document date.
**Attributes:** `type` (→ `data-date-type`, e.g. publication), `format`.

```html
<date data-date-type="publication">2024-03-15</date>
```

<## #el:config | `<config>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<config>` · **content** structured. Document-
level settings, written as keyword attributes and placed at the document's end.
**Attributes (live):** `citation-style`, `number-sections`, `number-equations` /
`number-figures` / `number-tables` / `number-theorems` / `number-definitions` /
`number-examples`, `counter-reset-scope`, `note-scope`, `note-position`
(`bottom` / `margin` — render numbered notes as side notes), `strict-mode`
(`off` / `sigil` / `canonical` — the strictness register switch, see
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:strict" | ch. 15>), and any `ref-prefix-*` key.
The settings are consumed by the pipeline; render-time options like
`embedResources` belong to the rendering API, not here (see
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:render" | ch. 14>).

```html
<config citation-style="apa" number-figures="false"></config>
```

<## #el:data | `<data>` ##>
**Category** metadata · **renders** `<data>` · **content** structured. The
apparatus container at the document's foot. Holds a `<library>` of bibliographic
sources (and, by convention, other edge apparatus).

```html
<data>
  <library data-format="bibtex"> @article{goodall2024, … } </library>
</data>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:library" | `<library>`>, <a href="#el:cite" | `<cite>`>.

<# #cat:inline | Inline elements #>

Formatting and inline semantics inside a paragraph. The formatting tags take a
same-line long form (`<b>bold</b>`) and a pipe form (`<b | bold>`); several have
markdown idioms. See <a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:inline" | Authoring Guide
ch. 3>.

<## #el:b | `<b>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<b>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose. Bold /
strong-visual text. **Authoring:** `**bold**` · `<b>bold</b>` · `<b | bold>`.
**Attributes:** `type` (→ `data-bold-type`, an optional semantic tag like
`keyword`), `id`, `class`.

```html
<p>The keyword <b data-bold-type="keyword">recursion</b> is fundamental.</p>
```

<## #el:i | `<i>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<i>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose. Italic
text. **Authoring:** `*italic*` or `_italic_` · `<i>italic</i>` · `<i | italic>`.
**Attributes:** `type` (→ `data-italic-type`, e.g. `taxonomic`).

```html
<p>The species is <i data-italic-type="taxonomic">Loxodonta africana</i>.</p>
```

<## #el:s | `<s>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<s>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose.
Strikethrough. **Authoring:** `~~struck~~` · `<s>struck</s>` · `<s | struck>`.
**Attributes:** `type` (→ `data-strike-type`).

```html
<p>The price is <s>$50</s> now $40.</p>
```

<## #el:u | `<u>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<u>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose. Underline
(no markdown form). **Authoring:** `<u>under</u>` · `<u | under>`. **Attributes:**
`type` (→ `data-underline-type`, e.g. `misspelling`).

```html
<p>The author wrote <u data-underline-type="misspelling">recieve</u>.</p>
```

<## #el:q | `<q>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<q>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose. A short
inline quotation (a browser adds quotation marks). **Authoring:** `<q>quoted</q>` ·
`<q | quoted>`. **Attributes:** `cite` (source URL).

```html
<p>She said <q>hello</q> in passing.</p>
```

<## #el:a | `<a>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<a>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose. A
hyperlink or anchor. The URL is the first positional argument or the `href` kwarg;
the text is the body. Markdown `[text](url)` is *not* supported. **Authoring:**
`<a URL | text>` · `<a href="URL" | text>` · `<a href="URL">text</a>`.
**Attributes:** `href`, `target`, `rel`, `title`.

```html
<p>See <a href="https://example.com/docs">the documentation</a>.</p>
```

<## #el:code | `<code>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<code>` (HTML-native) · **content** opaque.
Inline code; content is verbatim. **Authoring:** `` `code()` `` · `<` code() `>`.
**Attributes:** `language`.

```html
<p>The function is <code>factorial</code>.</p>
```

<## #el:inline-math | `<inline-math>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<inline-math>` · **content** opaque (LaTeX). Math
set within a run of text, rendered by KaTeX. **Authoring:** `$…$` · `<$ … $>`.

```html
<inline-math> … KaTeX-rendered HTML of the LaTeX source … </inline-math>
```

<## #el:sup | `<sup>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<sup>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose.
Superscript. **Authoring:** `^{text}` · `<sup | text>`.

```html
<p>The 1<sup>st</sup> edition.</p>
```

<## #el:sub | `<sub>` ##>
**Category** inline · **renders** `<sub>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose.
Subscript. **Authoring:** `_{text}` · `<sub | text>`.

```html
<p>Water is H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>
```

<# #cat:block | Block elements #>

Standalone blocks between paragraphs. See
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:block" | Authoring Guide ch. 4>.

<## #el:blockquote | `<blockquote>` ##>
**Category** block · **renders** `<blockquote>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose.
A quotation set off from the body. **Authoring:** `> quote` (markdown) ·
`<blockquote>quote</blockquote>` · `<blockquote | quote>` (the alias `<quote | …>`
also works). The markdown form wraps its content in a `<p>`.

```html
<blockquote>A short quotation.</blockquote>
```

<## #el:list | `<list>` ##>
**Category** block · **renders** `<ul>` / `<ol>` + `<li>` (HTML-native) ·
**content** items. A list. The item marker has three forms — the canonical
`<li>`, the sigil `<->` / `<*>`, and the markdown `-` / `*` — and all lower to the
same output. This is the inaugural **HTML-shaped Layer 1** case: the vocabulary
keys `list` / `li` for authoring, but the rendered Layer 1 is plain
`<ul>` / `<ol>` / `<li>` (see <a href="design.html" | Design> for the model).
**Authoring:** `<list>` with `<li>` items; `<list ordered>` renders `<ol>`.
**Attributes:** `ordered` boolean, `marker=` (CSS `list-style-type`), `start`,
`reversed`. See <a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:block" | Authoring Guide ch. 4>.

```html
<ul>
  <li>An item.</li>
  <li>Another item.</li>
</ul>
```

<## #el:code-block | `<code-block>` ##>
**Category** block · **renders** `<pre><code>` · **content** opaque. A fenced code
block with optional language. The vocabulary key is `code-block`, but the rendered
output is `<pre><code class="language-…">`. **Authoring:** a triple-backtick fence
with a language, or the sigil `<``` lang | code ```>`. There is no `<code-block>`
named tag to author directly.

```html
<pre><code class="language-python">def f(x):
    return x + 1
</code></pre>
```

<## #el:display-math | `<display-math>` ##>
**Category** block · **renders** `<display-math>` · **content** opaque (LaTeX).
Centered, numbered display math. **Authoring:** a `$$ … $$` fence (delimiters on
their own lines) or the sigil `<$$ | … $$>`; a single-line `$$x$$` is inline.
**Attributes:** `numbered` boolean, `id` (`#eqn:name`).

```html
<display-math> … KaTeX-rendered HTML … <span class="equation-number">(1)</span></display-math>
```

<# #cat:math | Math environments #>

LaTeX-style multi-row math, rendered by KaTeX as display blocks. Content is verbatim
LaTeX; use the long form. All share the equation counter.

<## #el:align | `<align>` ##>
**Category** block (math) · **renders** `<align>` · **content** opaque. An aligned
multi-line equation (LaTeX `aligned`). **Authoring:** `<align> … </align>`.

```html
<align> … KaTeX-rendered HTML of \begin{aligned}…\end{aligned} … </align>
```

<## #el:eqnarray | `<eqnarray>` ##>
**Category** block (math) · **renders** `<eqnarray>` · **content** opaque. As
`<align>`, for LaTeX-source compatibility. `<eqnarray> … </eqnarray>`.

<## #el:cases | `<cases>` ##>
**Category** block (math) · **renders** `<cases>` · **content** opaque. A
case-analysis brace (LaTeX `cases`). `<cases> x & x \ge 0 \\ -x & x < 0 </cases>`.

<## #el:matrix | `<matrix>` ##>
**Category** block (math) · **renders** `<matrix>` · **content** opaque. A matrix
(LaTeX `matrix`). `<matrix> 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 </matrix>`.

<# #cat:frame | Frameable elements #>

Blocks that share one mechanism for captions, automatic numbering, and an optional
border. At render time they gain a numbered label (`Figure 1.`, `Table 1.`). See
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:fig" | Authoring Guide chs. 5–6>.

<## #el:fig | `<fig>` ##>
**Category** frameable · **renders** `<figure>` · **content** prose (the caption).
A numbered, captioned figure. `<figure>` is an authoring alias for `<fig>`. The
caption is the pipe content (also the `<img>` `alt`). **Authoring:**
`<fig src="…" | caption>` · `<fig src="…" />` (captionless). **Attributes:** `src`,
`alt`, `align`, `width` (→ `data-width`; no separate height), `type`, `caption`;
booleans `numbered`, `border`. **Id:** `#fig:name`.

```html
<figure>
  <img src="elephant.jpg" alt="An adult African elephant." />
  <figcaption>An adult African elephant.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

*Related:* <a href="#el:table" | `<table>`>, <a href="#el:ref" | `<ref>`>.

<## #el:table | `<table>` ##>
**Category** frameable · **renders** `<table>` (HTML-native) · **content**
opaque-or-structured. A numbered, captioned data table. **Authoring:**
`<table csv | …>` / `<table tsv | …>` (a `csv`/`tsv` positional qualifier names the
data format), the standalone shorthands `<csv | …>` / `<tsv | …>` (gate-expanded to
`<table csv>` / `<table tsv>`), or a markdown pipe table. **Attributes:** `caption`,
`src`, `type`; booleans `headers`, `numbered`. **Id:** `#tab:name`.

```html
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td>foo</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
```

<## #el:svg | `<svg>` ##>
**Category** frameable · **renders** `<svg>` (HTML-native) · **content** opaque
(raw SVG). An inline vector graphic sharing the frameable surface. **Attributes:**
`width`, `height`, `viewBox`, `caption`; booleans `numbered`, `border`.

The SVG markup is the element's (long-form) content and passes through verbatim;
the `width`/`height`/`viewBox` kwargs land on the rendered `<svg>`.

```html
<svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" width="200" height="200">
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" fill="blue" />
</svg>
```

<## #el:frame | `<frame>` ##>
**Category** frameable · **renders** `<figure class="frameable-border">` · **content** prose. A generic
frameable callout — a captioned, optionally bordered box for content that is not a
figure, table, or diagram. The custom name `<frame>` is the authoring key; the
handler emits a `<figure>` (border on by default). **Attributes:** `title`,
`caption`, `type`; booleans `numbered`, `border` (default on).

```html
<figure class="frameable-border">A short callout.</figure>
```

<## #el:aside | `<aside>` ##>
**Category** frameable · **renders** `<aside data-aside-type="…">` · **content** prose.
Tangential content, and the home for **callouts / admonitions**: a `type` of `note`,
`info`, `tip`, `warning`, or `caution` is styled per-type by the default theme (accent
stripe, background tint, and a CSS icon — color is never the only cue); the plain `sidebar`
/ `callout` types get the box with no admonition styling. `<aside>` shares the frameable
surface — `title` (rendered on top), `caption` (bottom), `border` (**on** by default) —
and a numbered `<aside>` joins its own "Box N" counter (not the figure counter).
**Attributes:** `type`, `title`, `caption`; booleans `numbered`, `border`.

```html
<aside data-aside-type="warning" class="frameable-border">
  <p class="title">Heads up</p>
  Do not run this on production data.
</aside>
```

<# #cat:thm | Theorem family #>

Formal statements in numbered, styled blocks. `<theorem>`, `<lemma>`,
`<corollary>`, and `<proposition>` share one counter; `<definition>` and
`<example>` each have their own; `<remark>` and `<proof>` are unnumbered. All accept
pipe, same-line long, and multi-line long forms, a `name=` kwarg, and an `#id`. See
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:thm" | Authoring Guide ch. 10>.

<## #el:theorem | `<theorem>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<theorem>` · **content** prose. A
formally-stated assertion; opens the shared theorem counter. **Attributes:** `name`
(→ `data-name`, the "(Pythagoras)" label suffix); boolean `numbered`. **Id:**
`#thm:name`.

```html
<theorem>If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.</theorem>
```

<## #el:lemma | `<lemma>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<lemma>` · **content** prose. A
supporting result; shares the theorem counter. As `<theorem>` otherwise.
`<lemma>Every continuous function on a compact set attains its maximum.</lemma>`

<## #el:corollary | `<corollary>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<corollary>` · **content** prose. A
consequence; shares the theorem counter.
`<corollary>Every prime greater than 2 is odd.</corollary>`

<## #el:proposition | `<proposition>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<proposition>` · **content** prose. A
minor result; shares the theorem counter.
`<proposition>The sum of two even integers is even.</proposition>`

<## #el:definition | `<definition>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<definition>` · **content** prose. A
defined term; on its own counter (Definition 1, 2, …).
`<definition name="Group">A set with an associative operation, identity, and inverses.</definition>`

<## #el:example | `<example>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<example>` · **content** prose. A worked
instance; on its own counter.
`<example>The integers under addition form a group.</example>`

<## #el:remark | `<remark>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<remark>` · **content** prose. An aside;
*unnumbered* (rendered "Remark.").
`<remark>The converse does not hold in general.</remark>`

<## #el:proof | `<proof>` ##>
**Category** theorem-family · **renders** `<proof>` · **content** prose. A proof;
*unnumbered* (rendered "Proof."), a peer of the result it follows, not a child.

```html
<theorem>The sum of two even integers is even.</theorem>
<proof>Let $a = 2m$ and $b = 2n$. Then $a + b = 2(m+n)$. $\square$</proof>
```

<# #cat:cite | Citation and bibliography elements #>

Citations key into a `<library>` of bibliographic sources; the formatting is
delegated to a citation engine. See
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:cite" | Authoring Guide ch. 7>.

<## #el:cite | `<cite>` ##>
**Category** citation · **renders** `<cite>` (HTML-native) · **content** prose. An
in-text citation, by key. **Authoring:** `<cite @key>` · `<cite @key1, @key2>`
(grouped). **Attributes:** `keys`, `page`, `chapter`, `section`, `prefix`,
`suffix`, `style`.

```html
<p>… supported by data <cite class="cite" data-keys="goodall2024">(Goodall 2024)</cite>.</p>
```

<## #el:library | `<library>` ##>
**Category** citation · **renders** `<library>` · **content** opaque (BibTeX /
CSL-JSON). The bibliographic source, inside `<data>`. **Authoring:**
`<library | @article{…}>` (inline) or `<library src="refs.bib">` (external).
**Attributes:** `format`, `src`.

```html
<library data-format="bibtex"> @article{goodall2024, author = {Goodall, Jane}, … } </library>
```

<## #el:bibliography | `<bibliography>` ##>
**Category** citation · **renders** `<bibliography>` · **content** structured. Marks
where the formatted reference list renders (end of an article; back matter in a
book). Authored empty: `<bibliography></bibliography>`. **Attributes:** `source`,
`style`, `sort`, `type`.

```html
<bibliography><h2>References</h2><div class="csl-bib-body"> … </div></bibliography>
```

<# #cat:note | Footnote elements #>

<## #el:note | `<note>` ##>
**Category** footnote · **renders** `<note>` (canonical) · **content** prose. A
footnote, written in pipe form flush against the word it annotates. **Authoring:**
`text<note | …>`. At render time it splits into an inline `<sup>` marker (with a
hover preview on supporting surfaces) and a collected list at the document's foot.
**Attributes:** `placement`, `position`, `type`.

```html
Some text<note id="note-1">A substantive note about the text.</note>.
```

With `note-position=margin` in `<config>`, a numbered note renders as a *side
note* — its body set in the margin beside the marker instead of collected at the
foot — with no change to the note tree, its number, or its JATS. Below the margin
breakpoint it falls back to the foot-of-document list.

<## #el:marginnote | `<marginnote>` ##>
**Category** footnote · **renders** `<aside class="enscribe-marginnote">` ·
**content** prose. An *unnumbered* margin aside, authored in place — the Tufte
margin note, distinct from a numbered `<note>`. It is never numbered, collected,
or back-referenced; it floats into the same margin column as side notes (and
falls back to an inline-block aside on narrow screens). **Authoring:**
`text<marginnote | …>`. **Attributes:** `id`.

```html
The result holds.<aside class="enscribe-marginnote">A caveat, set in the margin.</aside>
```

<# #cat:ref | Cross-reference elements #>

<## #el:ref | `<ref>` ##>
**Category** cross-reference · **renders** `<a class="ref">` · **content** none. A
cross-reference to a numbered target by its typed id; renders the right word and
number ("figure 1", "theorem 2"), resolved after the whole document is parsed.
**Authoring:** `<ref @fig:flow>` (the `@`-prefixed id; the `#` form is *not* a
reference). Prefixes: `fig:`, `tab:`, `eqn:`, `thm:`/`lem:`/`cor:`/`prop:`, `def:`,
`ex:`, `sec:`. **Attributes:** `target`, `type`, `format`; booleans `link`,
`preview`. Sections are unnumbered in a flat article, so a `<ref @sec:…>` there
renders the id tail rather than a number.

```html
See <a href="#fig:flow" class="ref">figure 1</a> for details.
```

*Related:* <a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:xref" | Authoring Guide ch. 9>.

<# #cat:dsl | DSL elements #>

Embedded foreign languages — diagrams and music — held verbatim and drawn by an
external library. Content is opaque; use the long form. See
<a href="authoring-guide.html#sec:dsl" | Authoring Guide ch. 11>.

<## #el:diagram | `<diagram>` ##>
**Category** DSL host · **renders** `<pre class="ENGINE" data-enscribe-dsl="ENGINE">` ·
**content** opaque (engine source). The host for embedded diagram engines: a `mermaid` or
`abc` positional names the engine — `<diagram mermaid | …>`, `<diagram abc | …>`. Adding an
engine is a one-line registry entry, not a new element. **Attributes:** `caption`. Frameable;
numbers as a figure.

```html
<pre class="mermaid" data-enscribe-dsl="mermaid">graph TD
  A --> B</pre>
```

<## #el:mermaid | `<mermaid>` ##>
**Category** DSL · **renders** `<pre class="mermaid">` · **content** opaque (Mermaid
source). A diagram; `<mermaid>` is the standalone shorthand for `<diagram mermaid>`. The
source renders client-side (`live-link` / `live-inline`) or is shown verbatim (`skip`);
Mermaid has no static mode. **Attributes:** `caption`. A DSL block is frameable and numbers
as a figure.

```html
<pre class="mermaid" data-enscribe-dsl="mermaid">graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}</pre>
```

<## #el:abc | `<abc>` ##>
**Category** DSL · **renders** `<pre class="abc">` · **content** opaque (ABC source).
Music notation; `<abc>` is the standalone shorthand for `<diagram abc>`. Renders
client-side in the live modes, or to SVG at build time in `static` mode. **Attributes:**
`caption`.

```html
<pre class="abc" data-enscribe-dsl="abc">X:1
T:Twinkle, Twinkle
K:C
C C G G | A A G2 |</pre>
```

<# What this reference covers #>

This catalog documents the user-facing Layer 1 vocabulary — the elements authors
write and the HTML they compile to. Internal pipeline nodes, error markers, and
purely generated list structures (the footnote list, the rendered bibliography
entries) are omitted; they appear in the output but are never authored. For the
shorthand that produces these elements and worked examples of each, read the
<a href="authoring-guide.html" | Authoring Guide>; for the architecture behind the
canonical-form model, the <a href="design.html" | Design article>; and for how
Layer 1 exports to archival XML, the <a href="jats.html" | JATS article>.

